The Mersenne Twister (MT19937) is the most widely used Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG) in the world. It powers standard random functions in Python, PHP, and Ruby. It has an incredibly massive period (2¹⁹⁹³⁷-1), meaning it takes a long time before its sequence naturally repeats. [1, 2, 3] 
However, it is completely insecure for security applications. Because its output is generated using entirely linear operations (bitwise shifts and XOR), an attacker does not even need to guess the original seed. [2, 4, 5] 
If an elite hacker can capture 624 consecutive outputs, they can use simple bitwise algebra to invert the math, clone the internal state, and perfectly predict every single future number. [1, 2, 6] 
