## 🧮 The Mathematics: How It Works
The Mersenne Twister keeps an internal array of 624 integers. When you ask the system for a random number, it grabs one of these integers and processes it through a tempering function to randomize the bits before showing it to you. [1, 7, 8] 
The tempering math consists of four specific bitwise shifts and XOR masks: [7] 

   1. $y = y \oplus (y \gg u)$
   2. $y = y \oplus ((y \ll s) \ \& \ b)$
   3. $y = y \oplus ((y \ll t) \ \& \ c)$
   4. $y = y \oplus (y \gg l)$

Because these operations are linear, they are reversible. An attacker can write an untamper function to undo these shifts step-by-step and recover the raw internal state value.
