## 🛡️ How to Make Code Immune to State Tracking
To secure software against state recovery, developers must use a Cryptographically Secure PRNG (CSPRNG). CSPRNGs utilize non-linear, cryptographically strong functions (such as AES or ChaCha20) to generate bits. Even if you capture billions of outputs, computing the internal state remains mathematically impossible. [2, 9] 
## Secure Alternative Implementations:

* 
* In Python: Switch from random to the secrets module.

import secretssecure_token = secrets.token_hex(32) # Uses system entropy; completely un-predictable

* In Node.js / JavaScript: Avoid Math.random(). Use the built-in crypto module.

const crypto = require('crypto');const secureToken = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');

* In Linux/Bash: Read directly from /dev/urandom which pools physical hardware noise. [2] 
